   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter"> <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>Dear Miss Weaver: I am scribbling this in a post office as there is no ink in <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">my flat</seg>. I got the second cheque also. Many thanks. A polite clerk here cashed it without advice. I shall reply to <rs type="letter" key="ID_MISSING">your letter</rs> about <name type="person" key="quin70">Mr Quinn</name> <seg xml:id="seg2" type="note-anchor">tomorrow</seg>. I stopped overnight in <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_LYON">Lyon</name> and telephoned to <name type="person" key="dara82">Mr Darantière</name> to meet me on the platform at <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_DIJO">Dijon</name>. He took <seg xml:id="seg3" type="note-anchor">the same view</seg> as I did about the charges against <rs type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">the second edition</rs>. <rs type="person" key="joyc05">My son</rs> has been making <seg xml:id="seg4" type="note-anchor">inquiries</seg> for me and I shall let you know the result tomorrow and also <seg xml:id="seg5" type="note-anchor">my interview</seg> with <name type="person" key="beac87">Miss Beach</name>.</p>        	<p>Wonderful to say I have not had <seg xml:id="seg6" type="note-anchor">another relapse</seg>.</p>
                    <closer>
                        <salute>With kindest regards</salute>                               	<salute>sincerely yours</salute>                    	                    	<signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">James Joyce</name>
                        </signed>
                        <dateline>
                            <date when="1922-11-16">16/xi/922</date>
                        </dateline>
                    </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">From 1 November 1922, JJ lived at 26 Avenue Charles Floquet in Paris.</note>
                <note xml:id="note2" target="#seg2" type="commentary">As promised, JJ wrote <rs type="organisation" key="L_68947">to Weaver the following day</rs> (17 November 1922; <hi rend="italic">
                        <name type="work">LI</name>
                    </hi> 195). </note>
                <note xml:id="note3" target="#seg3" type="commentary">Following Sylvia Beach’s anger regarding the second printing of <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">Ulysses</name> and fear of legal troubles as a result of its similarity to her first edition, JJ consulted Darantiere. In his letter to Weaver of 17 November 1922, he makes their view explicit: “The second edition is differentiated in size and weight and is plainly marked a second edition in two places for any buyer who can read” (<hi rend="italic">
                        <name type="work">LI</name>
                    </hi> 196).</note>
                <note xml:id="note4" target="#seg4" type="commentary">After Beach’s fear that Paris booksellers would boycott <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">Ulysses</name> after the second edition’s publication, JJ’s seventeen-year-old son Giorgio visited prominent bookstores, as JJ wrote to Weaver, “with a couple of sinister looking friends making oblique enquiries.” Giorgio reported “no sign of any angry attitude about edition” among any booksellers  (<hi rend="italic">
                        <name type="work">LI</name>
                    </hi> 197).</note>
                <note xml:id="note5" target="#seg5" type="commentary">JJ briefly met with Beach to discuss these matters on 15 November 1922 and again a few days later, “to see whether the storm is abating or not after the blessed oil of catechumens which I poured out upon the surface of the waters” (<hi rend="italic">
                        <name type="work">LI</name>
                    </hi> 197). </note>
                <note xml:id="note6" target="#seg6" type="commentary">Following treatment in Nice by Dr Colin, JJ’s eyes were in an improved condition.</note>
            </div>
      </body>
